Here is a simple, portable C utility that prints the current date and time in ISO 8601 format (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS`).

### Prerequisites
You will need a standard C compiler (like `gcc`). No external libraries are required for this implementation, as it uses standard C99 features.

### The C Code (`iso_time.c`)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

int main(void) {
    time_t now;
    struct tm *local_time;
    char buffer[32];

    // Get the current time
    time(&now);

    // Convert time_t to struct tm (local time)
    local_time = localtime(&now);

    // Format the date and time into ISO 8601 string
    // %Y: 4-digit year
    // %m: 2-digit month (01-12)
    // %d: 2-digit day (01-31)
    // %H: 2-digit hour (00-23)
    // %M: 2-digit minute (00-59)
    // %S: 2-digit second (00-59)
    // %T: Shortcut for %H:%M:%S
    strftime(buffer, sizeof(buffer), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S", local_time);

    // Print the result
    printf("%s\n", buffer);

    return 0;
}
```

